\label{sec:introduction}
The escalating demand for wireless communication has driven the development of numerous technologies, yet the finite nature of the spectrum has created a growing shortage. This scarcity has led to inefficient spectrum utilization, prompting the concept of spectrum sharing. This paper presents a new framework for cognitive radio networks that employs a non-cooperative game-theoretic approach to achieve fair sub-carrier allocation. The proposed model utilizes a secondary user equipped with a multi-carrier modulation scheme, where sub-carrier assignment is determined by the user's channel state information. By applying the Nash Bargaining Solution, the proposed method ensures equitable resource distribution among secondary users. Simulation results indicate that the proposed approach yields a superior sum rate relative to other existing methods. Additionally, the model demonstrates resilience to imperfect channel sensing, delivering performance comparable to scenarios involving perfect sensing. The paper concludes by validating the scheme through simulations using a network topology typical of real-world environments, thereby confirming the practicality of the proposed approach. The source code is available at https://github.com/abhishekgupta1994/NonCooperativeGame.

